  "source": "JoyPro curated on the maker's photo (2026-10-11), seen straight from the front (pilot side), six levers on top, six toggle switches on the front face",
  "mappingSource": "CH quick start 300-133 \"Throttle Quadrant Button Assignments\" (https://www.chproducts.com/files/chproducts/Retail-Quickstarts/300-133%20Throttle%20Quadrant%20-%20FS2020.pdf): text: X = 1st lever, Y = 2nd, Z = 3rd, Y rotation = 4th, X rotation = 5th, Z rotation = 6th; buttons 1-12 toggle switches up/down (figure: 1/2 first switch ... 11/12 sixth); buttons 13-24 detents (figure: number pairs 13/14 ... 23/24 drawn beside the lever arrows from left to right, no own arrows)",
  "confidence": "high",
  "note": "The quick start figure's axis arrows are offset by one lever against its own text (the \"X Axis\" arrow points left of the first lever, \"Y Axis\" at the 1st, \"Z Axis\" at the 2nd lever ...), and the figure's boxes run X Rotation before Y Rotation while the text says Y Rotation = 4th lever, X Rotation = 5th lever. The catalog follows the text (1st..6th lever from the left; lever 4 = RY, lever 5 = RX). Lever positions are certain, the axis per lever is medium. The knob colours are interchangeable (quick start), so the lever is identified by its slot position, not by colour. Which button of a toggle pair is up and which is down is not documented; both buttons of a switch sit on the same toggle (odd marker slightly left, even slightly right, NOT meaning up/down). Detents (13-24) are lever positions at the end of travel (seen in Control Manager), not separate controls; they are kept as hidden candidates at the lever slots.",
